.SS Abstract
These macros execute the \f[CR]mfspr\f[R] instruction to read the
contents of \f[B]Special\-Purpose Register (SPR) 287\f[R] on PowerPC
processors.
.PP
\f[CR]mfspr\f[R] transfers the value of a specified SPR into a
general\-purpose register (GPR), allowing software to inspect processor
state or control information maintained in that register.
.PP
SPR \f[B]287\f[R] is implementation\-specific but is commonly associated
with \f[B]Processor Version Register (PVR)\f[R] on many PowerPC
variants.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]mfspr\f[R] is a privileged or semi\-privileged instruction on some
implementations; availability may depend on execution level.
.IP \(bu 2
The meaning of \f[B]SPR 287\f[R] is \f[B]implementation\-dependent\f[R],
though widely used as:
.RS 2
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Processor identification\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Version and revision encoding\f[R]
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
The returned value is typically structured as:
.RS 2
.IP \(bu 2
Upper bits: \f[B]Processor family / model\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
Lower bits: \f[B]Revision level\f[R]
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
The instruction performs a direct register transfer:
.RS 2
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]SPR[287] \-> GPR \-> variable\f[R]
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
Does not modify condition registers unless explicitly implemented by the
compiler backend.
.IP \(bu 2
Behavior and interpretation of SPR 287 may differ across:
.RS 2
.IP \(bu 2
Embedded PowerPC variants
.IP \(bu 2
Server\-class processors
.IP \(bu 2
Custom implementations
.RE
.PP
Software should not assume a fixed layout unless targeting a known
processor family.
